      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hey! No worries!\n\n\u003e Unfortunately, there\u0027s no easy way to see if the thread is gone and the pthread_t we are holding is no longer valid. The check you added isn\u0027t really expensive but it still leaves some window where we might crash. For example, let\u0027s say the thread has died after executing a couple of methods after the last snapshot. Then the check won\u0027t be sufficient since it appears as if the two methods were executed but the thread is dead and it isn\u0027t safe to access pthread_t structure.\n\nYes :/ I was thinking the same writing this. My hope was that this was fairly infrequent and we observe it only because it\u0027s a persistent state and so narrowing the race window would avoid most of the issues.\n\n\u003e If we do want to support cases where the thread dies before detaching then I was wondering if avoiding the use of pthread_t might be a better fix. It can be done but it isn\u0027t very clean. We basically need pthread_t to calculate the clockid_t in Thread::GetCpuNanoTime(). We can compute the clockid_t using the cached thread id. If we do that, we might still get incorrect results but we at least won\u0027t crash. However, that solution is not very clean and could break if bionic changes how it is computed. Unlikely that bionic would change but still not a clean solution. That\u0027s why I haven\u0027t implemented that.\n\nAlong those lines I was wondering about adding (or replacing `pthread_self` with) the `clockid_t` in `tlsPtr_`. That would avoid getting the clock at all during sampling.",
